\b\f35\fs36\cf8\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid14898233 CHAPTER ONE}{\insrsid15494165  }{\insrsid14898233 \line }{\insrsid15494165 The Activities of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14898233 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid15410282\charrsid14898233 
This chapter describes how King Priyavrata enjoyed royal opulence and majesty and then returned to full knowledge. King Priyavrata was detached from worldly opulence, and then he became attached to his}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid14898233  }{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid15410282\charrsid14898233 kingdom, but finally he again became detached from material enjoyment and thus achieved liberation. When King Par\'e9k\'f1it}{\fs28\cf1\dbch\af355\insrsid15410282\charrsid14898233 \hich\af35\dbch\af355\loch\f35  
}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid15410282\charrsid14898233 heard about this, he was struck with wonder, but he was somewhat bewildered as to how a devotee with no attachment for material enjoyment could later become attached to it. Thus in astonishment he questioned 
\'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 about this.
\par In response to the King's inquiries, \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said that devotional service, being transcendental, cannot be deviated by any material influences. Priyavrata had received transcendental knowledge from the}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid15553844  }{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid15410282\charrsid14898233 instructions of }{\fs28\cf1\insrsid15553844 N\'e4rada}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid15410282\charrsid14898233 
, and therefore he did not want to enter a materialistic life of enjoyment in a kingdom. He accepted the kingdom, however, at the request of such superior demigods as Lord Brahm}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid15553844\charrsid14898233 \'e4}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid15410282\charrsid14898233  and Lord Indra, the King of heaven.
\par Everything is under the control of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, the supreme controller, and everyone must work accordingly. Just as a bull is controlled by a rope tied to its nose, so all conditi}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid15553844 oned }{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid15410282\charrsid14898233 souls are forced to work under the spells of the modes of nature. A civilized man, therefore, works according to the institution of }{\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid15553844\charrsid15553844 var\'eba}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid15553844\charrsid15553844  and }{\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid15553844\charrsid15553844 \'e4\'e7rama}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid15410282\charrsid14898233 . Even in materialistic life, however, no one is free to act. Everyone is compelled to a
ccept a certain type of body offered by the Supreme Lord and thus be allotted different grades of happiness and distress. Therefore even if one artificially leaves home and goes to the forest, he again becomes attached to materialistic life. Family life i
s compared to a fortress for practicing sense control.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid14898233\charrsid14898233  When the senses are controlled}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid15410282\charrsid14898233  one}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid14898233\charrsid14898233  }{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid15410282\charrsid14898233 may live either at home or in the forest; there is no difference.
\par }\pard \s216\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14488765 {\insrsid14488765\charrsid14488765 When Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata, following the instruction of Lord Brahm\'e4}{\insrsid4025764 , a}{\insrsid14488765\charrsid14488765 
ccepted the royal throne, his father, Manu, left home for the forest. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata then married Barhi\'f1mat\'e9, the daughter of Vi\'e7vakarm\'e4. In the womb of Barhi\'f1mat\'e9 he begot ten sons, named \'c4gn\'e9dhra, Idhmajihva, Yaj\'efab
\'e4hu, Mah\'e4v\'e9ra, Hira\'ebyaret\'e4, Gh\'e5tap\'e5\'f1\'f6ha, Savana, Medh\'e4tithi, V\'e9tihotra and Kavi. He also begot one daughter, whose name was \'dcrjasvat\'e9. Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja Priyavrata lived with his wife and family for many thousands of years. The impressions from the rims of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata's chariot wheels created seven oceans and seven islands. Of the ten sons of Priyavrata, three sons named Kavi, Mah\'e4v
\'e9ra and Savana accepted }{\i\insrsid14488765\charrsid14488765 sanny\'e4sa}{\insrsid14488765\charrsid14488765 , the fourth order of life, and the remaining seven sons became the rulers of the seven islands. Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja Priyavrata also had a second wife, in whom he begot three sons named Uttama, Raivata and T\'e4masa. All of them were elevated to the post of Manu. \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 thus described how Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata achieved liberation.}{
