\par }\pard\plain \s304\qj \li0\ri0\sb600\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \v\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11612319 SB 5.1 Summary
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \v\f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11612319 
This chapter describes how King Priyavrata enjoyed royal opulence and majesty and then returned to full knowledge. King Priyavrata was de
tached from worldly opulence, and then he became attached to his kingdom, but finally he again became detached from material enjoyment and thus achieved liberation. When King Par\'e9k\'f1
it heard about this, he was struck with wonder, but he was somewhat bewildered as to how a devotee with no attachment for material enjoyment could later become attached to it. Thus in astonishment he questioned \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 about this.
\par In response to the King's inquiries, \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said that devotional service, being transcendental, cannot be deviated by any material influences. Priyavrata had received transcendental knowledge from the instructions of N\'e4
rada, and therefore he did not want to enter a materialistic life of enjoyment in a kingdom. He accepted the kingdom, however, at the request of such superior demigods as Lord Brahm\'e4 and Lord Indra, the King of heaven.
\par Everything is under the control of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, the supreme controller, and everyone must work accordingly. Just as a bull is contro
lled by a rope tied to its nose, so all conditioned souls are forced to work under the spells of the modes of nature. A civilized man, therefore, works according to the institution of }{\cs337\i\insrsid11612319 var\'eba}{\insrsid11612319  and }{
\cs337\i\insrsid11612319 \'e4\'e7rama.}{\insrsid11612319  Even in materialistic life, however, no one is free to
 act. Everyone is compelled to accept a certain type of body offered by the Supreme Lord and thus be allotted different grades of happiness and distress. Therefore even if one artificially leaves home and goes to the forest, he again becomes attached to m
aterialistic life. Family life is compared to a fortress for practicing sense control. When the senses are controlled. one may live either at home or in the forest; there is no difference.
\par When Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata, following the instruction of Lord Brahm\'e4. accepted the royal throne, his father, Manu, left home for the forest. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata then married Barhi\'f1mat\'e9, the daughter of Vi\'e7vakarm\'e4
. In the womb of Barhi\'f1mat\'e9 he begot ten sons, named \'c4gn\'e9dhra, Idhmajihva, Yaj\'efab\'e4hu, Mah\'e4v\'e9ra, Hira\'ebyaret\'e4, Gh\'e5tap\'e5\'f1\'f6ha, Savana, Medh\'e4tithi, V\'e9tihotra and Kavi. He also begot one daughter, whose name was 
\'dcrjasvat\'e9. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata lived with his wife and family for many thousands of years. The impressions from the rims of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata's chariot wheels crea
ted seven oceans and seven islands. Of the ten sons of Priyavrata, three sons named Kavi, Mah\'e4v\'e9ra and Savana accepted }{\cs337\i\insrsid11612319 sanny\'e4sa,}{\insrsid11612319 
 the fourth order of life, and the remaining seven sons became the rulers of the seven islands. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata also had a second wife, in whom he begot three sons named Uttama, Raivata and T\'e4
masa. All of them were elevated to the post of Manu. \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 thus described how Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata achieved liberation.
\par }\pard\plain \s304\qj \li0\ri0\sb600\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \v\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11612319 SB 5.1.1
