 had persuaded his son Priyavrata to take the responsibility for ruling the world. Priyavrata and N\'e4rada were also very satisfied. Although Brahm\'e4 had forced Priyavrata to accept the management of worldly affairs, thus breaking his vow to remain }{
\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 brahmac\'e4r\'e9}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  and completely engage in devotional service, N\'e4rada and Priyavrata did not look upon Brahm\'e4 with resentment. N\'e4
rada was not at all sorry that he had been frustrated in making Priyavrata a disciple. Both Priyavrata and N\'e4rada were exalted personalities who knew how to respect Lord Brahm\'e4. Therefore instead of looking upon Brahm\'e4 with resentme
nt, they very feelingly offered him their respect. Lord Brahm\'e4 then returned to his celestial abode, known as Satyaloka, which is described here as being impeccable and being unapproachable by words.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 It is stated in this verse that Lord Brahm\'e4 returned to 
his residence, which is as important as his own personality. Lord Brahm\'e4 is the creator of this universe and the most exalted personality within it. His lifetime is described in }{\cs347\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  (}{\cf1\insrsid11612319 8.17}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 ). }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Sahasra-yuga-paryantam ahar yad brahma\'ebo vidu\'f9.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  The tota
l duration of the four yugas is 4,300,000 years, and when that is multiplied a thousand times, it equals twelve hours in the life of Brahm\'e4. Therefore we cannot factually comprehend even twelve hours of Brahm\'e4
's life, to say nothing of the one hundred year
s that constitute his entire lifetime. How, then, can we understand his abode? The Vedic literatures describe that in Satyaloka there is no birth, death, old age or disease. In other words, since Satyaloka is situated next to Brahmaloka, or the Brahman ef
fulgence, it is almost as good as Vaiku\'eb\'f6haloka. Lord Brahm\'e4's abode is practically indescribable from our present status. Therefore it has been described as }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 av\'e4\'ec-manasa-gocara,}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  or beyond the description of our words and the imagination of our minds. The Vedic literatures thus describe the abode of Lord Brahm\'e4: }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 yad vai par\'e4rdhya\'e0 tad up\'e4rame\'f1\'f6hya\'e0
 na yatra \'e7oko na jar\'e4 na m\'e5tyur n\'e4rtir na codvega\'f9.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  "In Satyaloka, which is situated many millions and billions of years away, there is no lamentation, nor is ther
e old age, death, anxiety or the influence of enemies."
\par }\pard\plain \s304\qj \li0\ri0\sb600\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \v\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11612319 SB 5.1.22
