There is a Bengali saying which describes that someone is so powerful that he can make the night day and the day night. That saying is current because of the prowess of Priyavrata. His activities demonstrate how powerful
 he became by worshiping the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba is known as Yoge\'e7vara, the master of all mystic powers. In }{\cs347\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  (}{\cf1\insrsid11612319 
18.78}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 ) it is said wherever there is the master of all mystic powers (}{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 yatra yoge\'e7vara\'f9 k\'e5\'f1\'eba\'f9}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 ), victory, fortun
e and all other opulences are present. Devotional service is so powerful. When a devotee achieves what he wants to accomplish, it is not by his own mystic power but by the grace of the master of mystic power. Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a: by His grace, a devotee can accomplish wonderful things unimaginable even to the most powerful scientist.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
From the description in this verse, it appears that the sun moves. According to modern astronomers, the sun is fixed in one place, surrounded by the solar system, but here we find}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid5274697  that the sun is not stationary;}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  it is rotating in a prescribed orbit. This fact is corroborated by }{\cs347\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Brahma-sa\'e0hit\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  }{\cf1\insrsid11612319 (5.52)}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 . }{
\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Yasy\'e4j\'efay\'e4 bhramati sa\'e0bh\'e5ta-k\'e4la-cakra\'f9:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  the sun is rotating in its fixed orbit in accordance with the order of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. According to }{
\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Jyotir Veda,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
 the science of astronomy in the Vedic literature, the sun moves for six months on the northern side of the Sumeru Hill and for six months on the southern side. We have practical experience on this planet that when there 
is summer in the north there is winter in the south and vice versa. Modern materialistic scientists sometimes present themselves as knowing all the ingredients of the sun, yet they are unable to offer a second sun like Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata's.
\par Although Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata devised a very powerful chariot as brilliant as the sun, he had no desire to compete with the sun-god, for a Vai\'f1\'ebava never wants to supersede another Vai\'f1\'eb
ava. His purpose was to give abundant benefits in material existence. \'c7r\'e9la Vi\'e7van\'e4tha Cakravart\'e9 \'d6h\'e4kura remarks that in the months of April and May the rays of Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja Priyavrata's brilliant sun were as pleasing as the rays of the moon, and in October and November, both morning and evening, that sun provided more warmth than the sunshine. In short, Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja Priyavrata was extremely powerful, and his actions extended his power in all directions.
\par }\pard\plain \s304\qj \li0\ri0\sb600\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \v\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11612319 SB 5.1.31
