bhajann apakvo 'tha patet tato yadi\line yatra kva v\'e4bhadram abh\'fcd amu\'f1ya ki\'e0\line ko v\'e4rtha \'e4pto 'bhajat\'e4\'e0 sva-dharmata\'f9
\par }\pard\plain \s31\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
"One who has forsaken his material occupations to engage in the devotional service of the Lord may sometimes fall down while in
 an immature stage, yet there is no danger of his being unsuccessful. On the other hand, a nondevotee, though fully engaged in occupational duties, does not gain anything." If one somehow or other comes to the shelter of a great Vai\'f1\'ebava, takes to K
\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness because of sentiment or realization, but in course of time falls down because of immature understanding, he is not actually fallen, for his having engaged in K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a consciousness is a permanent asset. If one falls down, therefore, his progress might be checked for a certain time, but it will again become manifest at an opportune moment. Although Priyavrata Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja was serving according to the instructions of N\'e4rada Muni meant for going back home, back to Godhead, he returned to material affairs at the request of his father. In due course of time, however, his consciousness for serving K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a reawakened by the grace of his spiritual master, N\'e4rada.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 As stated in }{\cs347\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  (}{\cf1\insrsid11612319 6.41}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 ), }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 \'e7uc\'e9n\'e4\'e0 \'e7r\'e9mat\'e4\'e0 gehe yoga-bhra\'f1\'f6o 'bhij\'e4yate.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  One who falls down 
from the process of }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 bhakti-yoga}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  is again offered the opulence of the demigods, and after enjoying such material opulence, he is given a chance to take birth in a noble family of a pure }{
\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 br\'e4hma\'eba,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  or in a rich family, to be given the chance to revive his K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness. This actually happened in the life of Priyavrat}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid9202356 a;}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  he is a most glorious example of this truth. In due course of time, he no longer wanted to enjoy his material opulences and his wife, kingdom and sons; instead, he wanted to renounce them 
all. Therefore, after having described the material opulences of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata, \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, in this verse, describes his tendency for renunciation.
\par The words }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 devar\'f1i-cara\'eb\'e4nu\'e7ayana}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  indicate that Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata, having fully surrendered to the great sage Devar\'f1i N\'e4
rada, was strictly following all the devotional processes and regulative principles under his direction. In regard to strictly following the regulative principles, \'c7r\'e9la Vi\'e7van\'e4tha Cakravart\'e9 \'d6h\'e4kura says; }{
\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 da\'eb\'f2avat-pra\'eb\'e4m\'e4s t\'e4n anupatita\'f9.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  By immediately offering obeisances (}{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 da\'eb\'f2avat}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
) unto the spiritual master and by strictly following his directions, the student becomes advanced. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata was doing all these things regularly.
\par As long as one is in the material world, he has to be under the influence of the modes of material nature (}{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 gu\'eba-visarga}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 ). It is not that Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja Priyavrata was freed from material influence because he possessed all material opulences. In this material world, both the very poor man and the very rich 
man are under material influences, for both wealth and poverty are creations of the modes of material nature. As stated in }{\cs347\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  (}{\cf1\insrsid11612319 3.27}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 ), }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 prak\'e5te\'f9 kriyam\'e4\'eb\'e4ni gu\'ebai\'f9 karm\'e4\'ebi sarva\'e7a\'f9.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  According to the modes of material nature we acquire, the materia
l nature gives us facility for material enjoyment.
\par }\pard\plain \s304\qj \li0\ri0\sb600\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \v\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11612319 SB 5.1.37
