How condemned is the advancement of material knowledge can be understood from the behavior of Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja Priyavrata. He performed such wonderful acts as creating another sun, which shined during the night, and creating a chariot so great that its wheels form
ed vast oceans. These activities are so great that modern scientists cannot even imagine how such things can be done. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata acted very wonderfully in the material field of activities, but because he was dealing in sense gratification
\emdash ruling his kingdom and dancing to the indications of his beautiful wife\emdash he personally condemned himself. When we think about this example of Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja Priyavrata, we can just consider how degraded is the modern civilization of materialistic advancement. Modern so-c
alled scientists and other materialists are very satisfied because they can construct great bridges, roads and machines, but such activities are nothing comparable to those of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata. If Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja Priyavrata could condemn himself in spite of hi
s wonderful activities, how condemned we are in our so-called advancement of material civilization. We can conclude that such advancement has nothing to do with the problems of the living entity entangled within this material world. Unfortunately, modern 
m
an does not understand his entanglement and how condemned he is, nor does he know what kind of body he is going to have in the next life. From a spiritual point of view, a great kingdom, beautiful wife and wonderful material activities are all impediments
 to spiritual advancement. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Priyavrata had served the great sage N\'e4rada sincerely. Therefore even though he had accepted material opulences, he could not be deviated from his own task. He again became K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a conscious. As confirmed in }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4:
