abhadeva was popular with the general populace and the governmental servants, he chose to install Him on the imperial throne. In addition, he wanted to entrust his son into the hands of the learned }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 br\'e4hma\'ebas.}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  This means that a monarch was supposed to govern strictly according to Vedic principles under the guidance of learned }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 br\'e4hma\'ebas,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  who could advise
 Him according to the standard Vedic scriptures like }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Manu-sm\'e5ti}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  and similar }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 \'e7\'e4stras.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
 It is the duty of the king to rule the citizens according to Vedic principles. According to Vedic principles, society is divided into four categories\emdash }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 br\'e4hma\'eba, k\'f1atriya, vai\'e7ya}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  and }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 \'e7\'fcdra.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 C\'e4tur-var\'ebya\'e0 may\'e4 s\'e5\'f1\'f6a\'e0 gu\'eba-karma-vibh\'e4ga\'e7a\'f9.}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  After dividing society in this way, it is the king's duty to see that everyone executes Vedic principles according to his caste. A }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
 must perform the duty of a }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  without cheating the public. It is not that one attains the name of a }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
 without the qualifications. It is the king's duty to see that everyone engages in his occupational duty according to Vedic principles. In addition, retirement at the end of life is compulsory. Mah\'e4r\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid13465108 ja N\'e4
bhi, although still a king,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  retired from family life and went with his wife to a place called Badarik\'e4\'e7rama in the Himalayas, where the Deity Nara-N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba is worshiped. The words }{
\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 prasanna-nipu\'ebena tapas\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  indicate that the King accepted all ki
nds of austerity very expertly and jubilantly. He did not at all mind leaving his comfortable life at home, although he was the emperor. Despite undergoing severe austerities and penances, he felt very pleased at Badarik\'e4\'e7
rama, and he did everything there expertly. In this way, being fully absorbed in K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness (}{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 sam\'e4dhi-yoga}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid2510454 ),}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  always thinking of K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, V\'e4sudeva, Mah\'e4r\'e4ja N\'e4bhi attained success at the end of his life and was promoted to the spiritual world, Vaiku\'eb\'f6haloka.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 This is the way of Vedic l
ife. One must stop the process of repeated birth and death and return home, back to Godhead. The words }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 tan-mahim\'e4nam av\'e4pa}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  are significant in this regard. \'c7r\'e9la \'c7r\'e9dhara Sv
\'e4m\'e9 says that }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 mahim\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  means liberation in this life. We should act in such a way in t
his life that after giving up this body, we will become liberated from the bondage of repeated birth and death. This is called }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 j\'e9van-mukti.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  \'c7r\'e9la V\'e9rar\'e4ghava \'c4c\'e4
rya states that in the }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Ch\'e4ndogya Upani\'f1ad}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  there are eight symptoms of a }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 j\'e9van-mukta,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  a person w
ho is already liberated even when living in this body. The first symptom of one so liberated is that he is freed from all sinful activity (}{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 apahata-p\'e4pa}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
). As long as one is under the clutches of }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 m\'e4y\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  in the material energy, one has to engage in sinful activity. }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  describes such people as }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 du\'f1k\'e5tina\'f9,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
 which indicates that they are always engaged in sinful activity. One who is liberated in this life does not commit any sinful activities. Sinful activity involves illicit sex, meat-eating, intoxicatio
n and gambling. Another symptom of a liberated person is }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 vijara,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  which indicates that he is not subjected to the miseries of old age. Another symptom is }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
vim\'e5tyu.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  A liberated person prepares himself in such a way that he does not take on any more material bodies, which are destined to die. In other words}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4025764 , h}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
e does not fall down again to repeat birth and death. Another symptom is }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 vi\'e7oka,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  which indicates that he is callous to material distress and happiness. Another is }{
\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 vijighatsa,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  which indicates that he no longer desires material enjoyment. Another symptom is }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 apip\'e4t\'e4,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
 which means that he has no desire other than to engage in the devotional service of K\'e5\'f1\'eba, his dearmost pursuable Lord. A further symptom is }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 satya-k\'e4ma,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
 which indicates that all his desires are directed to the Supreme Truth, K\'e5\'f1\'eba. He does not want anything else. He is }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 satya-sa\'eckalpa.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
 Whatever he desires is fulfilled by the grace of K\'e5\'f1\'eba. First of all, he does not desire anything for his material benefit, and secondly if he desires anything at all
, he simply desires to serve the Supreme Lord. That desire is fulfilled by the Lord's grace. That is called }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 satya-sa\'eckalpa.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  \'c7r\'e9la Vi\'e7van\'e4tha Cakravart\'e9
 points out that the word }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 mahim\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  means returning to the spiritual world, back home, back to Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha. \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva says that the word }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
mahim\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  means that the devotee attains the qualities of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. This is called }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 sadharma,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  or "the same quality." Just as K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a is never born and never dies, His devotees who return to Godhead never die and never take birth within the material world.
\par }\pard\plain \s304\qj \li0\ri0\sb600\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \v\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11612319 SB 5.4.6
