a Bharata, being completely freed from the bodily conception, did not protest; he proceeded carrying the palanquin. When he continued as before, the King threatened him with punishment, and being threatened by the King, Ja\'f2
a Bharata began to talk. He protested against the filthy language used by the King when the King chastised him, and the King, hearing the instructions of Ja\'f2
a Bharata, was awakened to his real knowledge. When he came to his consciousness, he understood that he had offended a great, learned and saintly person. At that time he very humbly and respectfully prayed to Ja\'f2
a Bharata. He now wanted to understand the deep meaning of the philosophical words used by Ja\'f2a Bharata, and with great sincerity, he begged his pardon. He admitted that if one offends the lotus feet of a pure devotee, he is certainly punished by
