"One becomes attracted to his body, home, property, children, relatives and wealth. 
In this way one increases life's illusions and thinks in terms of 'I and mine.' " The attraction for material things is certainly due to illusion. There is no value in attraction to material things, for the conditioned soul is diverted by them. One's life
 is successful if he is absorbed in the attraction of K\'e5\'f1\'eba's strength, beauty and pastimes as described in the Tenth Canto of }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  The M\'e4y\'e4v\'e4d\'e9
s are attracted to merging into the existence of the Lord, but K\'e5\'f1\'eba is more attractive than the desire to merge. The word }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 abhava\'f9}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
 means "not to take birth again in this material world." A devotee doesn't care whether he is going to be reborn or not. He is simply satisfied with the Lord's service in any condition. That is real }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 mukti.
\par }\pard\plain \s302\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9320371 \i\f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid11612319 \'e9h\'e4 yasya harer d\'e4sye\line karma\'eb\'e4 manas\'e4 gir\'e4\line 
