\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid13582514 \v\f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\insrsid13582514\charrsid13582514 In this chapter the descendants of Bharata Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja and many other kings are described. The son of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Bharata was named Sumati. He followed the path of liberation given by \'c5\'f1
abhadeva. Some people mistakenly thought Sumati to be the direct incarnation of Lord Buddha. The son of Sumati was Devat\'e4jit, and his son was Devadyumna. Devadyumna's son was Parame\'f1\'f6h\'e9, and his son was Prat\'e9ha. Prat\'e9ha was a very 
great devotee of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, and he had three sons, named Pratihart\'e4, Prastot\'e4 and Udg\'e4t\'e4. Pratihart\'e4 had two sons, Aja and Bh\'fcm\'e4. The son of Bh\'fcm\'e4 was Udg\'e9tha, and the son of Udg\'e9tha was Prast\'e4
va. The son of Prast\'e4va was Vibhu, and the son of Vibhu was P\'e5thu\'f1e\'eba, whose son was Nakta. The wife of Nakta, Druti, gave birth to Gaya, who was a very famous and saintly king. Actually King Gaya was a partial incarnation of Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u, and because of his great devotion to Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu he received the title Mah\'e4puru\'f1a. King Gaya had sons named Citraratha, Sumati and Avarodhana. The son of Citraratha was the emperor Samr\'e4\'f6, and his son was Mar\'e9
ci, whose son was Bindu. Bindu's son was Madhu, and Madhu's son was V\'e9ravrata. V\'e9ravrata's two sons were Manthu and Pramanthu, and the son of Manthu was Bhauvana. The son of Bhauvana was Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4, and the son of Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4
 was Viraja, who glorified the whole dynasty. Viraja had one hundred sons and one daughter. Of these, the son named \'c7atajit became very famous.
