 tad bhavaty alpa-medhas\'e4m:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  men of meager intelligence desire temporary happiness. We have actually seen that one of the disciples of Bhaktisiddh\'e4nta Sarasvat\'e9 \'d6h\'e4
kura wanted to enjoy the property of his spiritual master, and the spiritual master, being merciful toward him, gave him the temporary property, but not the power to preach the cult of Caitanya Mah\'e4
prabhu all over the world. That special mercy of the power to preach is given to a devotee who does not want anything material from his spiritual master but wants only to serve him. The story of the demon R\'e4va\'eba illustrates this point. Although R
\'e4va\'eba tried to abduct the goddess of fortune S\'e9t\'e4dev\'e9 from the custody of Lord R\'e4macandra, he could not possibly do so. The S\'e9t\'e4dev\'e9 he forcibly took with him was not the original S\'e9t\'e4dev\'e9, but an expansion of }{
\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 m\'e4y\'e4,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  or Durg\'e4dev\'e9. As a result, instead of winning the favor of the real goddess of fortune, R\'e4va\'eba and his whole family were vanquished by the power of Durg\'e4dev\'e9 (}{
\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 s\'e5\'f1\'f6i-sthiti-pralaya-s\'e4dhana-\'e7aktir ek\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 ).
