yagarbha, entered the great, dull, material globe called the sun. This indicates that the theory held by so-called scientists that no one lives there is wrong. }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
 also says that K\'e5\'f1\'eba first instructed }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  to the sun-god (}{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 ima\'e0 vivasvate yoga\'e0 proktav\'e4n aham avyayam}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 ). Therefore the sun is not vacant. It is inhabited by living entities, and the predominating deity is Vair\'e4ja, or Vivasv\'e4
n. The difference between the sun and earth is that the sun is a fiery planet, but everyone there has a suitable body and can live there without difficulty.
\par }\pard\plain \s277\qj \li0\ri0\sb360\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Thus end the Bhaktivedant
a purports of the Fifth Canto, Twentieth Chapter, of the }{\cs341\i0\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  entitled, "Studying the Structure of the Universe."
\par }\pard\plain \s20\qj \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \v\f35\fs20\cf19\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11612319 SB 5.21: The Movements of the Sun
\par }\pard\plain \s73\qc \li0\ri0\sb540\sa540\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0\pararsid5116842 \shading10000\cfpat10 
\b\f35\fs36\cf8\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid5116842 CHAPTER TWENTY ONE\line }{\insrsid11612319 The Movements of the Sun
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid13582514 \v\f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\insrsid13582514\charrsid13582514 This chapter informs us of the movements of the su
n. The sun is not stationary; it is also moving like the other planets. The sun's movements determine the duration of night and day. When the sun travels north of the equator, it moves slowly during the day and very quickly at night, thus increasing the d
uration of the daytime and decreasing the duration of night. Similarly, when the sun travels south of the equator, the exact opposite is true}{\v0\insrsid3239357\charrsid3239357 \emdash }{\v0\insrsid13582514\charrsid13582514 
the duration of the day decreases, and the duration of night increases. When the sun enters Karka\'f6a-r\'e4\'e7i (Cancer) and then travels to Si\'e0ha-r\'e4\'e7i (Leo) and so on through Dhanu\'f9-r\'e4\'e7i (Sagittarius), its course is called Dak\'f1i
\'eb\'e4yana, the southern way, and when the sun enters Makara-r\'e4\'e7i (Capricorn) and thereafter travels through Kumbhar\'e4\'e7i (Aquarius) and so on through Mithuna-r\'e4\'e7i (Gemini), its course is called Uttar\'e4ya\'eb
a, the northern way. When the sun is in Me\'f1a-r\'e4\'e7i (Aries) and Tul\'e4-r\'e4\'e7i (Libra), the duration of day and night are equal.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid621505 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid13582514\charrsid621505 On M\'e4
nasottara Mountain are the abodes of four demigods. East of Sumeru Mountain is Devadh\'e4n\'e9, where King Indra lives, and south of Sumeru is Sa\'e0yaman\'e9, the abode of Yamar\'e4ja, the superintendent of death. Similarly, west of Sumeru is Nimlocan
\'e9, the abode of Varu\'eba, the demigod who controls the water, and north of Sumeru is Vibh\'e4var\'e9, where
 the demigod of the moon lives. Sunrise, noon, sunset and midnight occur in all these places because of the movements of the sun. Diametrically opposite the place where the sunrise takes places and the sun is seen by human eyes, the sun will be setting an
d passing away from human vision. Similarly, the people residing diametrically opposite the point where it is midday will be experiencing midnight. The sun rises and sets with all the other planets, headed by the moon and other luminaries.
