By His glance, the Supreme Personality of Godhead enables the modes of material nature to ac
t as the causes of universal creation, maintenance and destruction. The Supreme Soul is unlimited and beginningless, and although He is one, He has manifested Himself in many forms. How can human society understand the ways of the Supreme?
\par }\pard\plain \s280\qc \li0\ri0\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid12542146 \b\v\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\insrsid11612319 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s124\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 From Vedic literature we learn that when the Supreme Lord glances (}{
\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 sa aik\'f1ata}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 
) over the material energy, the three modes of material nature become manifest and create material variety. Before He glances over the material energy, there is no possibility of the crea
tion, maintenance and annihilation of the material world. The Lord existed before the creation, and consequently He is eternal and unchanging. Therefore how can any human being, however great a scientist or philosopher he may be, understand the ways of th
e Supreme Personality of Godhead?
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 The following quotations from }{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 Caitanya-bh
\'e4gavata}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  (}{\cs337\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 \'c4di-kha\'eb\'f2a,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319  1.48-52 and 1.58-69) tell of the glories of Lord Ananta:
\par }\pard\plain \s302\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11818543 \i\f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid11612319 ki brahm\'e4, ki \'e7iva, ki sanak\'e4di 'kum\'e4ra'\line vy\'e4sa, \'e7
uka, n\'e4rad\'e4di, 'bhakta' n\'e4ma y\'e4\'ecra
\par }\pard\plain \s31\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9535257 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid11612319 "Lord Brahm\'e4, Lord \'c7iva, the four Kum\'e4ras }{
